#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
cmdscribe-reconcile -- cross-check the two capture layers.

STUB. The checks below are the specification; they are not implemented
yet. Do not present its output as evidence until it is.

Why this exists: the shell layer is trivially bypassable
(`PROMPT_COMMAND=`) and its attribution is heuristic. The kernel layer
(auditd) is neither. Comparing them turns "we hope the shell layer
worked" into a measured number.

Checks
------
attribution   shell login=  vs  audit auid=        per session.
              Disagreement = bug in the ancestor walk, or evasion.
              Emits a fleet-wide agreement rate so the heuristic is
              empirically validated instead of merely asserted.
volume        per shell pid: audit execve count vs shell record count.
              Live shell, many execves, no records -> logger disabled.
silent-shell  auditd saw bash execve for a session but zero shell
              records -> hook never sourced, or removed.
tamper        audit records under key cmdscribe_tamper.
              This is the check that catches someone editing the hook.
builtin-gap   shell records for commands with no matching execve
              (cd, export, redirections). Expected, not an alert --
              reported so it is not mistaken for loss.

Design constraints
------------------
* Join on (boot_id, pid), NOT on timestamps. Clock granularity and the
  exec-vs-prompt ordering make per-command timestamp joins noisy.
* Compare per-session counts, not individual records. Coarse and
  reliable beats precise and crying.
* PID is reused across reboots; audit's ses= plus first-exec time
  disambiguates.

Inputs: /var/log/cmdscribe.log and `ausearch -k cmd -i --start <t>`.
"""

